ASLCP - web control panel for AllStarLink 3
===========================================

Open the web UI:      http://<this-host>:8080   (8080 is the default port; see "listen" in aslcp.conf)
Admin account:        sudo aslcp admin create   (shows in the install output as a setup link)
Setup link fallback:  http://<this-host>:<port>/#/setup?code=<code>, with the code from
                      /var/lib/aslcp/setup-code or "journalctl -u aslcp"
Lost password:        sudo aslcp admin reset-password <user>
Accounts:             sudo aslcp admin list
Configuration:        /etc/aslcp/aslcp.conf       (then: sudo systemctl restart aslcp)
Logs:                 journalctl -u aslcp -u aslcp-helper -u aslcp-dv

Services
  aslcp.service         the web server, runs as user "aslcp" (hardened, no capabilities)
  aslcp-helper.socket   socket-activated root helper; performs package updates and
  aslcp-helper.service  firewalld changes on request from the web server. It accepts
                        a fixed set of operations only, from root and user "aslcp".

  aslcp-dv.service      the digital voice engine (USRP <-> md380-emu, echo test
                        network), runs as user "aslcp-dv" with no capabilities and
                        loopback-only network access. It is installed but NOT enabled
                        by the package: "Digital modes" in the web UI turns it on.
                        State: /var/lib/aslcp-dv (config.json, mode 0600, written by the
                        helper). Control socket: /run/aslcp-dv/ctl.sock (user "aslcp"
                        is a member of group "aslcp-dv" to reach it).
                        Logs: journalctl -u aslcp-dv

Asterisk access
  ASLCP reads the AMI credentials from /etc/asterisk/manager.conf ("auto"). The
  aslcp user is added to group "asterisk". If that file is not group-readable:
      sudo chgrp asterisk /etc/asterisk/manager.conf
      sudo chmod 640 /etc/asterisk/manager.conf
      sudo systemctl restart aslcp
  Alternatively set explicit credentials in the [ami] section of aslcp.conf.

Firewall
  The web port is the one in "listen" in /etc/aslcp/aslcp.conf (default 8080).
  If firewalld is running, installing the package opens it: through the
  firewalld service "aslcp" for 8080, as a plain TCP port for any other port.
  Otherwise open the port yourself if you use another firewall.
  To pick another port (1024-65535, or 80): run the installer again with ASLCP_PORT (it also opens
  the port in firewalld):
      curl -fsSL https://w1jar.github.io/aslcp/install.sh | sudo ASLCP_PORT=8181 sh
  or edit "listen" and run  sudo systemctl restart aslcp , then open the port in
  your firewall (firewalld: sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=8181/tcp
  && sudo firewall-cmd --reload).
  Port 80 and no other ASL web UI: run the installer with ASLCP_TAKEOVER=1. It
  stops and disables Allmon3, the web server of the ASL web UIs (apache2,
  lighttpd or nginx), asl-web-ui and Supermon (those that exist and are on),
  never Asterisk, records them in /var/lib/aslcp/takeover.list and moves ASLCP
  to port 80:
      curl -fsSL https://w1jar.github.io/aslcp/install.sh | sudo ASLCP_TAKEOVER=1 sh
  Undo (ASLCP goes back to its old port, the recorded units are turned on):
      curl -fsSL https://w1jar.github.io/aslcp/install.sh | sudo ASLCP_TAKEOVER=undo sh

ASL3
  The installer installs asl3 and asl3-menu first if they are missing (it adds
  the AllStarLink apt repository from https://repo.allstarlink.org/public/ and
  checks its key). Debian 12/13 only; on another release it installs the
  panel only. To skip: ASLCP_ASL3=0
      curl -fsSL https://w1jar.github.io/aslcp/install.sh | sudo ASLCP_ASL3=0 sh
  ASL3 sends AllStarLink an anonymous usage report every day
  (asl-telemetry.timer). ASLCP leaves it on. To turn it off and mask it:
      curl -fsSL https://w1jar.github.io/aslcp/install.sh | sudo ASLCP_ASL_TELEMETRY=0 sh
  (ASLCP_ASL_TELEMETRY=1 restores it.) By hand:
      sudo systemctl disable --now asl-telemetry.timer
      sudo systemctl mask asl-telemetry.timer asl-telemetry.service

Updates
  Settings > Updates in the web UI (administrators), or the usual:
      sudo apt update && sudo apt install --only-upgrade aslcp
  The repository is added by the install script; see https://w1jar.github.io/aslcp/

Reporting bugs
  Settings > About > Report a bug (prefilled form), and Download diagnostics (admin)
  for a log file with IP addresses and secrets removed. https://github.com/w1jar/aslcp

Removal
  apt remove aslcp   keeps /var/lib/aslcp (users, favorites) and the user
  apt purge aslcp    deletes them, the digital voice engine state (/var/lib/aslcp-dv)
                     and user, the config history (/var/lib/aslcp-config-history)
                     and the digital modes state (/var/lib/aslcp-dvstate,
                     /var/lib/aslcp-dvstate-history); /etc/asterisk stays
